---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import DocLayout from '../components/DocLayout.astro';
import Notice from '../components/Notice.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
const playTesting = site.androidBetaDistribution === 'google-play-testing';
const toc = [
  { id: 'before', label: 'Before you start' },
  { id: 'mac', label: 'Install on the Mac' },
  { id: 'phone', label: 'Install on the phone' },
  { id: 'pair', label: 'Pair' },
  { id: 'permissions', label: 'Turn on features' },
  { id: 'mirroring', label: 'Mirroring' },
  { id: 'token', label: 'Your beta token' },
  { id: 'background', label: 'Staying connected' },
  { id: 'removing', label: 'Removing Sidekick' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Setup" description="Install Sidekick on your Mac and Android phone, pair them with a QR code, and turn on the features you want." path="/setup">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Setup" title="Install, pair, and choose what to share." lede="About ten minutes, once. These steps match the current beta build; each warning you’ll see is listed in order." />
  <DocLayout toc={toc}>
    <Notice tone="warning" title="This is a test build">
      {playTesting ? (
        <p>The Mac app isn’t notarized by Apple yet, so macOS warns you before opening it. The Android app installs from Google Play once you’ve joined the test. Never turn off Gatekeeper or System Integrity Protection — nothing here needs it.</p>
      ) : (
        <p>Neither app is signed by Apple or listed on Google Play yet, so both systems warn you before opening it. Never turn off Gatekeeper or System Integrity Protection — nothing here needs it.</p>
      )}
    </Notice>

    <h2 id="before">Before you start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A Mac on {site.minimumRequirements.mac}.</li>
      <li>An Android phone on {site.minimumRequirements.android}.</li>
      <li>Both on the same network — the same Wi-Fi, or the Mac joined to the phone’s hotspot.</li>
      <li>The files and beta token from your invitation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="mac">1. Install on the Mac</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the <code>.dmg</code> you were sent — the <em>arm64</em> one for Apple silicon, the other for Intel — and drag <strong>Sidekick</strong> into <strong>Applications</strong>. The Intel build hasn’t been run on an Intel Mac yet; if you have one, please say so in your report.</li>
      <li>Open Sidekick. macOS says it <strong>could not verify</strong> the app. Choose <strong>Done</strong>.</li>
      <li>Open <strong>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, find the message about Sidekick, click <strong>Open Anyway</strong>, enter your password and confirm. You only do this once.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>beta token</strong>: paste it and press <strong>Use this token</strong>, or press <strong>Open token file…</strong>.</li>
      <li>If macOS asks whether Sidekick may <strong>find devices on your local network</strong>, click <strong>Allow</strong> — that’s how it reaches your phone. Sidekick then shows <strong>Scan this with your phone</strong> and a code.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="phone">2. Install on the phone</h2>
    {playTesting ? (
      <ol>
        <li>On your phone, open the <strong>test opt-in link</strong> from your invitation, signed in with the same Google account you gave when you asked to join.</li>
        <li>Tap <strong>Become a tester</strong>, then follow the link to Sidekick in the Play Store and tap <strong>Install</strong>. It can take a little while after you’re added before the app appears.</li>
        <li>Updates during the beta arrive through the Play Store like any other app. To leave the test, use the same opt-in link.</li>
      </ol>
    ) : (
      <ol>
        <li>Open the <code>.apk</code> you were sent.</li>
        <li>Android asks you to allow that app to <strong>install unknown apps</strong>. Turn it on for that app only, go back, and tap <strong>Install</strong>.</li>
        <li>If Google Play Protect warns about an unknown developer, read it; install only if the file came from the person who invited you.</li>
      </ol>
    )}

    <h2 id="pair">3. Pair</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open Sidekick on the phone and tap <strong>Get started</strong>, then <strong>Continue</strong>.</li>
      <li>On <strong>Connect your Mac</strong>, tap <strong>Scan the code</strong> and allow the camera. The camera only reads the code, on the phone.</li>
      <li>Point it at the code on your Mac. The phone shows <strong>Paired with</strong> your Mac’s name; the Mac shows your phone as <strong>Connected</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The pairing code is a one-time secret for your two devices. Don’t share screenshots of it.</p>

    <h2 id="permissions">4. Turn on the features you want</h2>
    <p>Each feature asks for its own permission the first time you use it. Skip any you don’t want — the rest still work, and each screen says what’s missing. Three are switched on by hand in Android Settings rather than by a prompt:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Notification access</strong> — to show phone notifications on the Mac.</li>
      <li><strong>Accessibility service</strong> — to control the phone from the Mac while mirroring.</li>
      <li><strong>Screen capture</strong> — allowed each time mirroring starts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/permissions">Every permission and what it’s for</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="mirroring">Mirroring</h2>
    <p>On the Mac’s Overview, press <strong>Start mirroring</strong> (<kbd>⌘</kbd> <kbd>⇧</kbd> <kbd>M</kbd>). Android asks you to allow screen capture <strong>every time</strong> — that’s Android’s rule. To click and type into the phone, switch on Sidekick’s accessibility service when asked. Banking apps, protected video and the lock screen show as black.</p>

    <h2 id="token">Your beta token</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Access runs until the end date written in your token — {site.betaAccessDuration?.days} days from its start date. <strong>Settings › About › Beta access</strong> shows the date and days left.</li>
      <li>You’ll see one quiet notice 7 days before the end and one the day before. Reinstalling doesn’t change the date.</li>
      <li>When it ends, Sidekick shows a screen with a box for a new token. Nothing is deleted: Activity, Settings and received files stay.</li>
      <li>If Sidekick says your Mac’s date looks earlier than before, check <strong>System Settings › General › Date &amp; Time</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="background">Staying connected</h2>
    <p>While your Mac is awake and connected, Sidekick keeps the phone ready to answer it, with a visible notification on the phone. When the Mac sleeps or disconnects, that stops. If your phone closes Sidekick in the background, find <strong>Battery</strong> or <strong>Background usage</strong> for Sidekick in the phone’s settings and allow it.</p>

    <h2 id="removing">Removing Sidekick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Phone:</strong> uninstall Sidekick. Files it received stay in <code>Download/ConnectSuite</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Mac:</strong> quit Sidekick, move it to the Bin, delete <code>~/Library/Application Support/sidekick-desktop</code>, and in Keychain Access delete <em>Sidekick Safe Storage</em>. Received files stay in <code>~/Downloads/Sidekick</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Stuck? See <a href="/help">Help</a>.</p>
  </DocLayout>
</BaseLayout>
